# Installed to /etc/sudoers.d/skylapse by install.sh.
#
# The services run unprivileged. Several operations genuinely need root —
# writing a camera overlay into the boot config, installing the ZWO SDK or
# Tailscale, launching the updater outside the API's cgroup, restarting units,
# rebooting — and every one of them goes through a single script with a closed
# list of verbs.
#
# ONE ENTRY, ON PURPOSE. There used to be three more, spelling out the exact
# argument lists for systemd-run and systemctl. Both had drifted from the
# commands the code actually built — the systemd-run rule expected
# --unit=skylapse-update where the real command had --uid= and
# --working-directory= first, and the systemctl rule named three units where the
# updater restarts two. Neither matched, so `sudo -n` failed with "a password is
# required", and the in-app updater could not be started from the web UI on any
# install. Nothing caught it because the contract lived half in a Python list
# and half in a sudoers pattern, where no test on either side could see both.
#
# So: sudo authorises the script, and the script owns its own arguments. Adding
# a flag to a command can no longer break sudo, and every argument that reaches
# root is validated in one place — scripts/skylapse-admin — rather than by
# sudo's pattern matching.
@SKYLAPSE_USER@ ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: @SKYLAPSE_ROOT@/scripts/skylapse-admin
